#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every IBM Quantum&reg; QPU reports a set of properties that describe the "
"current performance of its qubits: relaxation times ($T_1$), coherence times"
" ($T_2$), readout errors, and one- and two-qubit gate errors. These numbers "
"matter in practice. The transpiler uses them to select the physical qubits "
"for your circuit, error mitigation techniques rely on them, and you might "
"use them to judge whether a device is working well enough for an "
"experiment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The reported properties come from calibration procedures that typically run "
"about once a day. Superconducting qubits, however, can drift on shorter "
"timescales, and transient defects (known as two-level systems) can "
"temporarily degrade a qubit that performed well at calibration time. In "
"addition, you often transpile a job well before it runs, so decisions based "
"on the reported properties can depend on outdated information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses the `BackendCharacterization` utility from [Qiskit Device"
" Benchmarking](https://github.com/qiskit-community/qiskit-device-"
"benchmarking), which combines experiment construction, job submission, and "
"curve fitting into a few calls. For roughly 70 seconds of QPU time, you "
"receive a backend object whose properties reflect the current state of the "
"device. You can compare these properties against the reported values or pass"
" the backend to other tools. The final example uses the refreshed values to "
"select physical qubits for a GHZ-state circuit and compares the results with"
" those of the same circuit compiled with the reported properties.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The characterization experiments {#the-characterization-experiments}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The suite measures five properties, each with a standard experiment:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Readout error** (`readout`): The experiment prepares each qubit in "
"$|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$ and immediately measures it. The probability of"
" a wrong readout gives the state preparation and measurement (SPAM) error.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Single-qubit gate error** (`rb_1q`): Randomized benchmarking (RB) runs "
"sequences of random single-qubit Clifford gates that ideally compose to the "
"identity. The decay of the survival probability with sequence length gives "
"the average error per gate, independent of SPAM errors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Two-qubit gate error** (`rb_2q`): This experiment applies the same RB "
"method to full layers of non-overlapping two-qubit gates that run "
"simultaneously, as in a [layer fidelity](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05933) "
"experiment. Because many gates run at once, the resulting error rates "
"include crosstalk effects and are closer to the error rates in a deep "
"circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**$T_1$** (`t1`): The experiment excites each qubit and measures it after "
"increasing delays. The decay of the excited-state population gives the "
"relaxation time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**$T_2$** (`t2`): A Hahn echo experiment puts each qubit in a superposition,"
" applies an echo pulse halfway through a delay, and measures how quickly the"
" qubit loses phase coherence. By default, the experiment uses a single echo."
" A later section of this tutorial demonstrates a multi-echo (CPMG-style) "
"variant.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Scope and limits of the refreshed properties {#scope-and-limits-of-the-"
"refreshed-properties}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keep the following points in mind when you use this workflow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The refreshed properties exist only in the backend object that the utility "
"returns to you. Nothing changes on the device itself or in the calibration "
"data that IBM Quantum reports to other users.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The measured values are a snapshot taken with a particular methodology. Some"
" of them, notably the layered two-qubit errors and the parallel single-echo "
"$T_2$ values, are measured differently from the reported calibration data, "
"so part of any gap that you observe comes from methodology rather than "
"drift. This tutorial identifies these cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The characterization itself costs QPU time (about 70 seconds for the full "
"suite on a 156-qubit Heron processor), which is the cost of current "
"information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The workflow follows the four steps of a Qiskit pattern:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem.** Choose the device and"
" the set of properties to measure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution.** The utility "
"builds the experiment circuits directly in the native gates of the device "
"and parallelizes them across the device.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives.** The experiments run as Sampler "
"jobs inside a batch.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format.** Fit "
"the measurement data into per-qubit error maps, refresh the backend, and "
"compare the measured properties against the reported ones.\n"
msgstr ""

