msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Setup {#setup}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Import the required libraries.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The characterization suite builds several experiments, submits multiple "
"jobs, and fits the results. This process can take a few minutes in total. "
"Enable INFO-level logging so that each stage prints its progress as it "
"runs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial does not include a simulator example. The workflow measures "
"the physical imperfections of a specific device: how quickly its qubits "
"relax and dephase, and how often its gates and readout fail. An ideal "
"simulator has none of these imperfections, so there is nothing to "
"characterize. If you attach a synthetic noise model to a fake backend, the "
"experiments only recover the values that you put in the model. For that "
"reason, this tutorial proceeds directly to hardware, in the four steps of a "
"Qiskit pattern.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Large-scale hardware example {#large-scale-hardware-example}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-"
"inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this workflow, the \"problem\" is the device itself. The classical inputs"
" are the QPU that you want to characterize and the list of properties to "
"measure. The quantum experiments are the characterization circuits that the "
"utility generates from these inputs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, select the least-busy available QPU with a minimum of 120 qubits. The"
" results in this tutorial were collected on `ibm_boston`, a Heron r3 "
"processor, on 6 October 2026.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, choose which properties to measure. You can pass any subset of the "
"following experiments:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`readout`: SPAM (state preparation and measurement) experiment\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`rb_1q`: isolated single-qubit randomized benchmarking\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`rb_2q`: simultaneous two-qubit randomized benchmarking from a layer "
"fidelity experiment\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`t2`: $T_2$ Hahn echo experiment (a single echo by default)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`t1`: $T_1$ relaxation experiment\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run the full suite to characterize the device completely. To save QPU time, "
"remove the entries for any properties that you do not need.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-"
"problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In most tutorials, this step is where you transpile your circuits. In this "
"tutorial, `BackendCharacterization` handles transpilation internally. It "
"builds the experiment circuits directly in the native gates of the device, "
"runs the single-qubit experiments on all qubits in parallel, and schedules "
"the two-qubit benchmarking over disjoint layers of the coupling map, so that"
" a small number of jobs cover the entire device. Because of this "
"parallelization, the whole suite uses only about 70 seconds of QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Initialize the class that manages the workflow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-"
"primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `run_experiments` method builds the circuits and submits them as "
"[Sampler](/guides/get-started-with-sampler) jobs. Run it inside a "
"[`Batch`](/guides/run-jobs-batch) context so that the jobs are scheduled "
"together on the QPU. The method returns after it submits all of the jobs. "
"You can also run the method outside a `Batch` or inside a `Session`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"You might see a warning that a backend was passed while a session context manager is open. This warning is expected, and you can safely ignore it. The layer fidelity experiment might also warn that it does not use the optional `xslow` gate. You can ignore this warning too, because the experiment uses supported standard single-qubit gates.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The method returns the submitted jobs as a dictionary with one entry per "
"experiment. Use this dictionary to track the jobs on the [IBM Quantum "
"Platform dashboard](/guides/monitor-job) or to debug them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Analyze the results {#analyze-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `analyze_results` method waits for the jobs to finish and then processes"
" the measurement data. It fits exponential decays to the $T_1$ and $T_2$ "
"data, fits survival-probability decays to the RB data, and computes "
"assignment matrices for readout. It returns a dictionary of *error maps*, "
"with one entry per measured property. Each error map assigns a measured "
"value to each qubit (or qubit pair).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The maps cover readout error, the single-qubit `x` and `sx` gate errors, the"
" layered two-qubit errors (`lf_error_map`, from the layer fidelity "
"experiment), and the $T_1$ and $T_2$ times in seconds.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Update the backend properties {#update-the-backend-properties}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `update_backend` method writes the measured values into the properties "
"of a copy of the backend and returns the copy. The original `backend` object"
" keeps the reported calibration data, so that you can compare the two sets "
"of properties, as shown in the next section. This update applies only to "
"your Python session. It does not change anything on the device or for other "
"users.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compare the measured properties against the reported ones {#compare-the-"
"measured-properties-against-the-reported-ones}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, plot the measured real-time properties against the values that the "
"backend reports. In each panel, the qubits (or qubit pairs) are sorted by "
"measured value, so the measured curve is smooth by construction. The scatter"
" of the reported values around this curve shows where the two data sets "
"disagree. The single-qubit RB results appear in one plot, because the "
"utility assigns the same measured error per gate to both the `x` and `sx` "
"gates. The axis limits are based on most of the data, so that a few extreme "
"outliers do not compress the plots. An annotation on each plot counts any "
"points that fall outside the range. To set the limits yourself, pass the "
"`ylim` argument.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-2.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-3.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-4.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Read these plots carefully, because not every gap between the two curves "
"means that the device has drifted:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Readout and single-qubit errors**: The measured values track the reported "
"ones for most qubits, but for a few qubits the reported value is far from "
"the measured curve. These localized disagreements can reflect the drift that"
" this workflow is designed to detect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**$T_1$**: The two data sets scatter around each other with no systematic "
"offset, which is consistent with natural fluctuation of $T_1$ over time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Two-qubit errors**: The measured values are systematically above the "
"reported ones. This difference is expected, because the reported values come"
" from isolated gates, while the layer fidelity experiment runs many gates "
"simultaneously and therefore includes crosstalk. The layered values are more"
" relevant for deep circuits, but they are not directly comparable to the "
"reported calibration data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**$T_2$**: The measured values are well below the reported ones for most "
"qubits. As with the two-qubit errors, methodology plays a large role, which "
"the next section examines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Inspect individual experiment results {#inspect-individual-experiment-"
"results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can also inspect the raw results of the individual characterization "
"experiments through the `experiment_data` property, which returns the "
"underlying [Qiskit Experiments](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-"
"experiments/) `ExperimentData` objects. For example, the following code "
"displays the measured $T_1$ decay curve of a single qubit. Use a curve like "
"this one to check the quality of a fit before you rely on the value that the"
" fit produced.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-28-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measure T2 with a dynamical decoupling train {#measure-t2-with-a-dynamical-"
"decoupling-train}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"By default, the $T_2$ experiment applies a single Hahn echo and measures all"
" qubits in parallel. As the previous comparison plot shows, this method can "
"produce noticeably lower $T_2$ values than the backend reports. One possible"
" cause is the number of echoes. The reported values might come from a "
"calibration that uses dynamical decoupling, which suppresses low-frequency "
"noise. To test this hypothesis, run a $T_2$-only characterization with a "
"larger value of `t2_num_echoes`, which applies a CPMG-style train of echo "
"pulses. In both cases, the delays represent the total free-evolution time, "
"so the fitted $T_2$ values are directly comparable to the single-echo "
"results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-31-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this run, the extra echoes barely changed the result. The 8-echo median "
"(77 &micro;s) is close to the single-echo median (75 &micro;s), and both "
"remain far below the reported median (300 &micro;s). The number of echoes "
"alone does not explain this gap. Other methodological differences also "
"contribute, such as details of the calibration procedure, and measurement of"
" all qubits in parallel instead of in isolation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Therefore, compare $T_2$ values (and layered two-qubit errors) across "
"methodologies with care. The refreshed values are a self-consistent "
"snapshot, taken under conditions close to those of a real workload. These "
"values are well suited to compare qubits with each other or to track a "
"device over time. A gap relative to the reported calibration data, however, "
"is not automatically evidence of drift.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run steps 1–4 in a single call {#run-steps-14-in-a-single-call}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For routine use, you rarely need the intermediate results. The "
"`run_and_update` method combines the previous steps (build, submit, fit, and"
" update) into a single call that returns the refreshed backend. It accepts "
"the same `experiments` list and forwards options such as `t2_num_echoes`. "
"You can also wrap it in a `Batch` or `Session` context.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"The following cell does not return until all jobs and post-processing are complete.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The refreshed `backend_updated` object is ready for the GHZ comparison in "
"the next section. Refresh the properties close to execution time to reduce "
"the effect of further device drift.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use refreshed properties for qubit selection {#use-refreshed-properties-for-"
"qubit-selection}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This optional example completes the workflow. It checks whether the "
"refreshed properties lead to better circuit results. It compiles the same "
"circuits twice, once with the reported properties and once with the "
"refreshed properties. Then it runs both versions and compares their outputs."
" This example uses the `backend` and `backend_updated` objects from the "
"previous sections, and it submits additional work to the QPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build the comparison circuits {#build-the-comparison-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prepare an $n$-qubit Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger (GHZ) state with a Hadamard"
" gate followed by a chain of CNOT gates, and measure every qubit. Ideally, "
"the outcomes $|0\\cdots0\\rangle$ and $|1\\cdots1\\rangle$ each have "
"probability 0.5. Because you measure every qubit, an error on any qubit in "
"the chain, including a readout error, reduces the probability of the ideal "
"outcomes. The measured outputs are therefore sensitive to the physical "
"qubits that the transpiler selects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refreshed properties tend to matter most at moderate sizes, where the "
"transpiler can choose from many candidate qubit chains. Very large circuits "
"use most of the device, so the transpiler has little room to avoid poorly "
"performing qubits. To save QPU time, reduce the list of circuit sizes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/selection-"
"circuits-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Transpile with reported and refreshed properties {#transpile-with-reported-"
"and-refreshed-properties}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The transpiler uses a `Target` to choose physical qubits and native "
"instructions. If you update only the backend properties, an existing target "
"can remain unchanged. Instead, copy the original target and explicitly apply"
" the refreshed errors and coherence times to the copy. The copy keeps the "
"original instruction durations and connectivity. If a coherence time is "
"unavailable for a qubit, keep its existing target value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A reported error of 1 means that the error is undefined, because "
"benchmarking of that qubit or gate has not succeeded for several days, and "
"the value is outdated (see [QPU information](/guides/qpu-information)). This"
" value does not necessarily mean that the gate is not working, but you "
"should treat the gate with caution. A single characterization run can still "
"fit a low error for such a gate, so that the gate appears to be one of the "
"best on the device. To be cautious, keep those entries at their reported "
"value so that the transpiler continues to avoid them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use the same optimization level and random seed for both compilations so "
"that the comparison controls for transpiler randomness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before you run the circuits, check how many physical qubits each refreshed "
"layout changes. If the two compilations select the same qubits, the "
"refreshed properties cannot make a difference for that circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Execute both sets of circuits {#execute-both-sets-of-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run both versions on the same QPU, with the same shot count and error "
"suppression settings. Interleave the reported and refreshed versions in the "
"submission, and repeat the comparison three times to observe the variation "
"between trials. The order of submission does not guarantee the order of "
"execution, and interleaving does not eliminate drift.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compare the output distributions {#compare-the-output-distributions}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute the Hellinger fidelity between each measured distribution and the "
"ideal GHZ distribution. A value of 1 means that the distributions match. "
"This metric measures agreement only in the computational basis. It does not "
"measure the coherence between $|0\\cdots0\\rangle$ and $|1\\cdots1\\rangle$,"
" so it is an upper bound on the GHZ state fidelity, not a certificate of "
"entanglement.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The plot shows the mean across trials. The error bars indicate one standard "
"deviation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-"
"properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/selection-"
"results-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this run, the refreshed properties changed some or all of the selected "
"qubits at every size, and the refreshed layouts gave a higher mean fidelity "
"for seven of the eight GHZ states. The largest gain was for the 25-qubit "
"state, where the mean fidelity increased from 0.629 to 0.694. The refreshed "
"layout scored higher in every trial for the 5-, 10-, 20-, 25-, 35-, and "
"40-qubit states. Not every size improved. For the 15-qubit state, the "
"refreshed layout was slightly worse (0.813 compared with 0.821). For the "
"30-qubit state, the difference (0.596 compared with 0.591) is within the "
"variation between trials.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Refreshed properties do not guarantee better results. The benefit depends on"
" how far the device has drifted since its last calibration. Also, the "
"characterization is itself a noisy snapshot. The transpiler can select a "
"qubit or gate that happens to measure well, even if that qubit or gate does "
"not perform better. Shot noise, further drift between characterization and "
"execution, and differences in characterization methodology also affect the "
"comparison. Use the measured change and its variation to decide whether "
"refreshed properties are useful for your workload.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next steps {#next-steps}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"tip\" title=\"Recommendations\">\n"
"- Apply the qubit-selection comparison to your own circuits and evaluate whether the benefit justifies the cost of characterization\n"
"- Learn how to access the reported calibration data in the [QPU information](/guides/qpu-information) guide\n"
"- Explore the individual characterization experiments in the [Qiskit Experiments documentation](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/)\n"
"- Browse additional device-level benchmarks in the [Qiskit Device Benchmarking repository](https://github.com/qiskit-community/qiskit-device-benchmarking)\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

